\title{Flow Value Lemma}
\date{2026-11-16T18:50:01Z}
\tag{algorithms}

\import{algorithms-macros}
\import{flow-macros}

\taxon{Lemma}
\p{Let #{f} be any [flow](00RZ) and let #{(A,B)} be any [#{s}-#{t} cut](00SO) in a [flow network](00RX) #{G}. Then the [net flow](00SR) of #{(A,B)} with respect to #{f} equals the [value](00S0) of #{f}. That is, ##{v(A,B) = v(f).}}

\proof{
\stmt{#{v(f)}}
\reason{=}{[Definition of flow value](00S0)}
\stmt{#{\fout(s)}}
\reason{=}{#{\fin(s) = 0} since #{s} must have indegree #{0}}
\stmt{#{\fout(s) - \fin(s)}}
\reason{=}{By definition of a [flow](00RZ), #{\fout(v) - \fin(v) = 0} for every #{v \in A} except for #{s}}
\stmt{#{\sum_{v \in A} (\fout(v) - \fin(v))}}
\reason{=}{See explanation below}
\stmt{#{\sum_{e : A \to B} f(e) - \sum_{e : B \to A} f(e)}}
\reason{=}{definition of [net flow](00SR)}
\stmt{#{v(A,B)}}

\p{The remaining step that needs explanation is the central one that goes from #{\sum_{v \in A} (\fout(v) - \fin(v))}
to #{\sum_{e : A \to B} f(e) - \sum_{e : B \to A} f(e)}. To see why this is true, consider all the edges with at least one endpoint in #{A}.
\ul{
  \li{If an edge has both endpoints in #{A}, its flow contributes twice to the first sum, once positively and once negatively, and the two cancel out. (Intuitively: moving stuff around within #{A} does not affect its net exports.)}
  \li{Edges leaving #{A} contribute positively to the sum.}
  \li{Edges entering #{A} contribute negatively to the sum.}
}
}
}

